  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

Two weeks ago we built a database explorer and put a tiny locator map on each building's detail page. Today we're making the map the main event.

We're going to build a full interactive map of shooting incidents in New York City using [MapLibre GL JS](https://maplibre.org/), an open-source mapping library, with free tiles from [OpenFreeMap](https://openfreemap.org/). The data comes from the NYPD's public dataset on NYC Open Data.

Before we write any code, we need to talk about what we're mapping and why it matters.

## Part 1: Mapping responsibly

The NYPD publishes data on every shooting incident in New York City, going back to 2006. Each record includes the date, time, location, borough, and whether the incident was fatal. It also includes latitude and longitude, which means we can plot every shooting on a map.

This is powerful data for journalism. It can reveal patterns — which neighborhoods are most affected, whether shootings are rising or falling, what time of day they happen. But it can also mislead if you're not careful.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/nypd-data-portal.png"
  alt="The NYPD Shooting Incident Data on the NYC Open Data portal, showing a table of incident records"
  chromeTitle="NYPD Shooting Incident Data (Historic)"
  chromeUrl="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Public-Safety/NYPD-Shooting-Incident-Data-Historic-/833y-fsy8"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

A dot map of shootings can be informative, but it can also stigmatize communities. When you put a cluster of red dots on a neighborhood, you are attaching a label to that place. People who live there will see their home defined by its worst moments.

Good data journalists think about this. They ask questions like: Does showing individual points serve the reader better than showing borough-level totals? Should we aggregate by precinct or neighborhood instead of plotting exact locations? Do we need to show the entire historical dataset, or would the most recent year tell a clearer story?

There's no single right answer. But the conversation matters, and we'll keep it in mind as we build.

For today's class, I've prepared a GeoJSON file of shooting incidents from the most recent complete year. We'll plot them on a map, add filters, and build popups that show details when you click an incident.

### Set up your project

Create a new repository from the class template. Call it `nyc-shootings-map`. Clone it, open it in VS Code, and run:

```bash
npm install
```

This time, we also need to install one additional package — the MapLibre GL JS library:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl
```

This is the first time we've installed an external package beyond what's in the template. The `npm install maplibre-gl` command downloads the library and adds it to your project's `package.json` file. When you run `npm install` in the future, it will be included automatically.

Now start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Download the data file I've posted in the class chat and save it as `src/lib/data/shootings.geojson`.

Open it and take a look. [GeoJSON](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeoJSON) is a standard format for encoding geographic data. It looks like regular JSON, but with a specific structure. Here's a simplified version of one record:

```json
{
  "type": "Feature",
  "properties": {
    "date": "2025-07-04",
    "time": "23:45:00",
    "borough": "BROOKLYN",
    "precinct": 75,
    "fatal": false
  },
  "geometry": {
    "type": "Point",
    "coordinates": [-73.8743, 40.6505]
  }
}
```

Each record is a "Feature" with two parts: `properties` (the data about the incident) and `geometry` (the location). The coordinates are longitude first, then latitude — the opposite of how most people think about it, and one of the most common mistakes new mappers make.

## Part 2: Your first map

Let's get a map on the page. We're going to build a Svelte component that wraps MapLibre GL JS.

Create `src/lib/components/Map.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
  import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

  let container;

  $effect(() => {
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: container,
      style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
      center: [-73.95, 40.75],
      zoom: 10
    });

    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

    return () => map.remove();
  });
</script>

<div class="map" bind:this={container}></div>

<style>
  .map {
    width: 100%;
    height: 500px;
  }
</style>
```

There's a new Svelte feature here: [`$effect`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect). It's similar to `$derived`, but instead of creating a value, it runs a block of code after the component renders. We need this because MapLibre requires a DOM element to exist before it can draw a map inside it.

Let's walk through the code:

`import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` brings in the mapping library we just installed. The second import loads its CSS, which is necessary for the map controls and popups to look right.

`let container` declares a variable that will hold a reference to the `<div>` element where the map will be drawn. The `bind:this={container}` attribute on the `<div>` connects this variable to the actual DOM element — so when Svelte renders the page, `container` will point to that `<div>`.

Inside `$effect`, we create a new `maplibregl.Map` with four options:
- `container` — the DOM element to draw the map in
- `style` — the URL for our map tiles. We're using [OpenFreeMap](https://openfreemap.org/), a free service that provides beautiful map tiles with no API key required. The `liberty` style gives us a clean, readable base map.
- `center` — the starting position of the map, as `[longitude, latitude]`. These coordinates center the map on Manhattan.
- `zoom` — how close the map starts. 10 shows the full city; 12 would zoom into a borough; 15 would show a neighborhood.

The `addControl` line adds zoom buttons to the map. The `return () => map.remove()` line cleans up the map when the component is removed from the page — this prevents memory leaks.

Now let's use the component. Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace everything with:

```svelte
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
</script>

<h1>NYC Shooting Incidents</h1>
<Map />
```

Save and check your browser. You should see a headline and an interactive map of New York City underneath it.

