  src="/screenshots/week-11/first-map.png"
  alt="A MapLibre map centered on New York City showing streets, parks, and water features with zoom controls in the top right"
  chromeTitle="NYC Shooting Incidents"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Drag the map. Zoom in and out. Double-click to zoom. This is a full interactive vector map running in your browser, using free tiles, with no API key. Try doing that with Google Maps.

## Part 3: Plotting the data

A map without data is just a pretty picture. Let's add our shooting incidents.

Update `Map.svelte` to accept the data as a prop and plot it on the map:

```svelte
<script>
  import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
  import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

  let { data } = $props();
  let container;

  $effect(() => {
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: container,
      style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
      center: [-73.95, 40.75],
      zoom: 10
    });

    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

    map.on('load', () => {
      map.addSource('shootings', {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: data
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'shootings-layer',
        type: 'circle',
        source: 'shootings',
        paint: {
          'circle-radius': 4,
          'circle-color': '#e74c3c',
          'circle-opacity': 0.6
        }
      });
    });

    return () => map.remove();
  });
</script>

<div class="map" bind:this={container}></div>

<style>
  .map {
    width: 100%;
    height: 500px;
  }
</style>
```

We've made three changes. First, we added `let { data } = $props()` so the component accepts GeoJSON data from its parent. Second, we wrapped the data logic in `map.on('load', ...)` — this is MapLibre's way of saying "wait until the base map is fully loaded before adding anything." Third, inside that handler, we added a source and a layer.

`map.addSource` tells MapLibre about our data. We give it a name (`'shootings'`) and tell it the data is in GeoJSON format.

`map.addLayer` tells MapLibre how to display that data. A [circle layer](https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/layers/#circle) draws a circle at each point. The `paint` object controls the appearance: a red circle, 4 pixels in radius, at 60% opacity. The partial opacity is important — it lets you see when multiple incidents overlap.

Now update `+page.svelte` to pass the data in:

```svelte
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
  import shootings from '$lib/data/shootings.geojson';
</script>

<h1>NYC Shooting Incidents</h1>
<Map data={shootings} />
```

Save and check your browser. You should see red dots scattered across the city.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/dots-on-map.png"
  alt="A map of New York City covered in semi-transparent red dots representing shooting incidents, with visible concentrations in the Bronx, northern Manhattan, Brooklyn, and Queens"
  chromeTitle="NYC Shooting Incidents"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Take a moment to zoom in and explore. The patterns are striking. Some neighborhoods are dense with incidents. Others have almost none. This is the power of mapping data — the geography tells a story that a table of numbers cannot.

## Part 4: Adding popups

When a reader sees a dot on the map, their first instinct is to click it. Let's reward that instinct by showing a popup with the incident details.

Add this code inside the `map.on('load', ...)` handler in `Map.svelte`, after the `addLayer` call:

```js
map.on('click', 'shootings-layer', (e) => {
  const feature = e.features[0];
  const props = feature.properties;
  const coords = feature.geometry.coordinates;

  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(coords)
    .setHTML(`
      <strong>${props.date}</strong><br>
      ${props.time}<br>
      ${props.borough}, Precinct ${props.precinct}<br>
      ${props.fatal ? '<span style="color: #e74c3c; font-weight: bold;">Fatal</span>' : 'Non-fatal'}
    `)
    .addTo(map);
});

map.on('mouseenter', 'shootings-layer', () => {
  map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer';
});

map.on('mouseleave', 'shootings-layer', () => {
  map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '';
});
```

The first block listens for clicks on the `shootings-layer`. When a user clicks a dot, MapLibre provides the features at that point. We take the first one, extract its properties, and create a popup showing the date, time, borough, precinct, and whether the incident was fatal.

The second and third blocks change the cursor to a pointer when hovering over a dot — a visual cue that it's clickable, just like a link.

Save and click on a dot. A popup should appear with the incident details.

