## Part 8: A choropleth view

The dot map shows every individual incident. That's powerful, but it can also overwhelm — a dense cluster of dots in one neighborhood blends together into a red blob that's hard to read. And as we discussed in Part 1, individual dots can stigmatize specific locations in ways that aggregated data does not.

A [choropleth map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choropleth_map) offers a different perspective. Instead of plotting individual points, it divides the map into regions and colors each region by a count or rate. Think of the election maps you see on TV — red and blue states shaded by vote margins. Same idea, applied to shootings by police precinct.

Our shooting data already has a `precinct` field, and the city publishes a GeoJSON file of NYPD precinct boundaries. I've prepared this file for you — download `precincts.geojson` from the class chat and save it to `src/lib/data/`.

### Aggregating the data

First, we need to count how many incidents happened in each precinct and attach that count to the precinct polygons. We'll do this in JavaScript when the page loads.

In `+page.svelte`, import the precinct boundaries and compute the counts:

```js
import precincts from '$lib/data/precincts.geojson';
```

Then build the aggregated data as a `$derived` value:

```js
let precinctData = $derived(() => {
  // Count incidents per precinct, respecting current filters
  let counts = {};
  for (let feature of shootings.features) {
    let p = feature.properties;
    if (boroughFilter !== 'all' && p.borough !== boroughFilter) continue;
    if (fatalOnly && !p.fatal) continue;
    counts[p.precinct] = (counts[p.precinct] || 0) + 1;
  }

  // Attach counts to precinct polygons
  let enriched = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(precincts));
  for (let feature of enriched.features) {
    let id = feature.properties.precinct;
    feature.properties.count = counts[id] || 0;
  }
  return enriched;
});
```

This loops through the shooting incidents (respecting whatever borough and fatal filters are active), tallies the count per precinct, and then copies the precinct GeoJSON and attaches a `count` property to each polygon. The `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...))` is a quick way to make a deep copy — we don't want to modify the original data.

### Creating a ChoroplethMap component

Create `src/lib/components/ChoroplethMap.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
  import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

  let { data } = $props();
  let container;
  let map;
  let loaded = $state(false);

  $effect(() => {
    map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: container,
      style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
      center: [-73.95, 40.75],
      zoom: 10
    });

    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

    map.on('load', () => {
      map.addSource('precincts', {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: data
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'precincts-fill',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'precincts',
        paint: {
          'fill-color': [
            'interpolate',
            ['linear'],
            ['get', 'count'],
            0, '#f7fbff',
            10, '#c6dbef',
            25, '#6baed6',
            50, '#2171b5',
            100, '#08306b'
          ],
          'fill-opacity': 0.7
        }
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'precincts-outline',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'precincts',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#333',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      });

      map.on('click', 'precincts-fill', (e) => {
        const props = e.features[0].properties;
        new maplibregl.Popup()
          .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
          .setHTML(`
            <strong>Precinct ${props.precinct}</strong><br>
            ${props.count} shooting incidents
          `)
          .addTo(map);
      });

      map.on('mouseenter', 'precincts-fill', () => {
        map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer';
      });

      map.on('mouseleave', 'precincts-fill', () => {
        map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '';
      });

      loaded = true;
    });

    return () => map.remove();
  });

  $effect(() => {
    if (!loaded) return;
    map.getSource('precincts').setData(data);
  });
</script>

<div class="map" bind:this={container}></div>

<style>
  .map {
    width: 100%;
    height: 500px;
  }
</style>
```

Most of this follows the same pattern as the dot map. The two key differences are in the layer configuration.

Instead of `type: 'circle'`, we're using `type: 'fill'`, which draws filled polygons. The paint expression is more complex — it uses MapLibre's [`interpolate`](https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/expressions/#interpolate) function to create a color ramp. Reading it from the inside out: get the `count` property from each polygon, and map it linearly across a blue color scale — from nearly white (0 incidents) through light blue (10) to dark navy (100+). This is the same kind of sequential color scale you'd see in any professional choropleth.

The second `$effect` watches for changes to the `data` prop and calls `setData` on the source. This is how the choropleth updates when the user changes a filter — instead of re-filtering a layer (like we did with the dots), we pass in a whole new GeoJSON object with updated counts.

### Adding it to the page

Let's give readers a way to toggle between the two views. In `+page.svelte`, add a state variable for the view mode and import the new component:

```js
import ChoroplethMap from '$lib/components/ChoroplethMap.svelte';

let viewMode = $state('dots');
```

Add a toggle control alongside the existing filters:

```svelte
<div class="controls">
  <select bind:value={boroughFilter}>
    <option value="all">All boroughs</option>
    <option value="BRONX">Bronx</option>
    <option value="BROOKLYN">Brooklyn</option>
    <option value="MANHATTAN">Manhattan</option>
    <option value="QUEENS">Queens</option>
    <option value="STATEN ISLAND">Staten Island</option>
  </select>

  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" bind:checked={fatalOnly} />
    Fatal incidents only
  </label>

  <div class="view-toggle">
    <button
      class:active={viewMode === 'dots'}
      onclick={() => viewMode = 'dots'}
    >Incidents</button>
    <button
      class:active={viewMode === 'precinct'}
      onclick={() => viewMode = 'precinct'}
    >By precinct</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

Use an `{#if}` block to swap between the two maps:

```svelte
{#if viewMode === 'dots'}
  <Map data={shootings} filter={mapFilter} />
{:else}
  <ChoroplethMap data={precinctData} />
{/if}
```

Style the toggle:

```css
.view-toggle {
  display: flex;
  border: 2px solid #ddd;
}

.view-toggle button {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border: none;
  background: white;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.view-toggle button.active {
  background: var(--color-accent);
  color: white;
}
```

Save and try it. Click "By precinct" and the map transforms from scattered dots into colored polygons. The filters still work — select a borough and the counts recalculate, shifting the colors across the precincts.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/choropleth.png"
  alt="A choropleth map of NYC showing police precincts shaded in varying intensities of blue, with darker precincts indicating more shooting incidents. A popup shows 'Precinct 75 — 87 shooting incidents.'"
  chromeTitle="NYC Shooting Incidents"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Click "Incidents" to switch back to the dots. The two views tell different stories from the same data. The dots show you where specific incidents happened — the human scale. The choropleth shows you which precincts carry the heaviest burden — the institutional scale. Offering both lets the reader choose the lens that serves their question.

Save, commit, and push. Check your live site.

## Part 9: Going further with AI

The map we built today is a solid foundation, but there's a lot more you can do. This is a good time to experiment with AI assistance. Try asking your coding assistant to:

- "Color the dots differently for fatal vs. non-fatal incidents."
- "Add a heatmap layer as a third view mode."
- "Cluster nearby incidents into groups that expand when you zoom in."
- "Add a color legend below the choropleth map that explains the scale."
- "Add a tooltip that shows the precinct number as I hover, without clicking."

MapLibre is a large library with many features, and its documentation can be dense. This is exactly the kind of situation where an AI assistant shines — it can translate your editorial intent ("I want to highlight fatal incidents") into the right API calls (`'circle-color': ['case', ['get', 'fatal'], '#e74c3c', '#3498db']`).

Try one or two of these experiments before committing your final version.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own interactive map

Find a dataset on the NYC Open Data portal or another public source that includes latitude and longitude coordinates. Build an interactive map for it.

Your project must have:

- A MapLibre map with data points plotted from a real dataset
- At least one filter or interactive control
- Click-to-inspect detail for individual points (popups)
- An editorial frame: headline, introductory text, and source credit

Some datasets that could work well:

- NYC WiFi Hotspot Locations (digital access)
- NYC Community Gardens (urban farming, equity)
- FDNY Response Times (emergency services)
- 311 Noise Complaints (quality of life — use a recent month to keep the size manageable)
- NYC Street Trees (environment — filter to a single borough)

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your map with the class and explain how you built it. You should be prepared to:

- Show the dataset you chose and explain what story the geography tells
- Walk through how you connected Svelte's reactive filters to MapLibre
- Show it on your phone — does the map work well on a small screen?
- Discuss what editorial choices you made about what to show and what to leave out
